course: biology
lesson: 358
title: Bones by name: shoulders, arms and hands
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Your arm is a chain of bones from the shoulder to the fingertips: the collarbone and shoulder blade at the top, one long upper arm bone, two forearm bones and then a hand packed with small bones.
explain: Swing your arm in a full circle: only the shoulder can do that, because it is a ball sitting in a shallow socket, free to turn almost any way, held there mostly by muscles.
explain: Now turn a door handle: your hand flips palm up and palm down because one forearm bone, the radius, rolls over the other, the ulna.
explain: Bang your elbow on a table and your little finger tingles: that is the funny bone, which is not a bone at all but a nerve lying close under the skin.
fact: The collarbone is one of the most commonly broken bones in children, often from falling on an outstretched arm, which sends the force up to the shoulder; it usually heals well in a few weeks.
fact: The shoulder blade, or scapula, is a flat triangular bone held mostly by muscles, gliding on the back so the arm can move widely.
fact: The shoulder is a ball-and-socket joint and the most mobile joint in the body; its socket is shallow, so it moves freely but can pop out, which is called dislocation.
fact: The upper arm bone is the humerus, and the funny bone is really a nerve running past the elbow, whose tingling runs down into the little finger; the name is a pun on humerus and humorous.
fact: The forearm has two bones, the radius on the thumb side and the ulna on the little-finger side; the radius rolls over the ulna when you turn your hand.
fact: Each hand has 27 bones: 8 in the wrist, 5 in the palm and 14 in the fingers, so both hands together have 54, about a quarter of all the body's bones.
fact: The thumb has two finger bones and the other fingers three each, and the thumb can touch every fingertip, which gives a strong, careful grip.
fact: A newborn's wrist bones are mostly soft cartilage that hardens one by one through childhood, so a doctor can estimate a child's bone age from a wrist X-ray; an adult's shows all eight.
fact: Cracking knuckles makes a pop as a gas bubble forms in the joint fluid; one doctor cracked the knuckles of one hand for 60 years and found no arthritis, a painful stiffness of the joints, and studies have not shown it to be harmful.
fact: A bat's wing has the same arm bones as ours, upper arm, forearm and very long finger bones, with skin stretched between the fingers; whales, birds and horses share the same plan too.
exercise: Which bone in the shoulder is often broken by falling on an outstretched arm? | The collarbone | The round kneecap | The back of the skull | The heel bone of the foot
exercise: What holds the shoulder blade in place? | Mostly muscles | A screw-like joint to the spine | The ribs, joined tight | Nothing, it floats freely in fat
exercise: What kind of joint is the shoulder? | A ball-and-socket joint | A hinge joint like a door | A fused joint that cannot move | A small gliding joint only
exercise: Why can a shoulder pop out of place more easily than a hip? | Its socket is shallow, so it moves freely but holds less tightly | It has no muscles around it at all to hold the bones together | Its bones are made of cartilage | It is the weakest bone in the body
exercise: What is the funny bone really? | A nerve near the elbow | The pointed end of the ulna | A tiny bone inside the elbow | A muscle in the forearm
exercise: Where does the tingling go when you hit your funny bone? | Into the little finger | Into the thumb | Up into the side of the neck | Down into the knee
exercise: What is the bone of the upper arm called? | The humerus | The radius | The femur | The ulna
exercise: How many bones are in the forearm? | Two | Just one | Three | Five
exercise: Which forearm bone is on the thumb side? | The radius | The ulna | The humerus | The scapula
exercise: What lets you turn a door handle? | The radius rolling over the ulna | The elbow spinning round | The wrist bones melting together | The shoulder blade turning
exercise: How many bones are in one hand? | 27 | 5 | 14 | 54
exercise: How many finger bones does a thumb have? | Two | Three | Only one | Four
exercise: How can a doctor estimate a child's bone age? | By looking at the wrist bones on an X-ray | By counting how many baby teeth the child has lost | By measuring the child's shadow | By weighing the child's arm
exercise: What makes the pop when you crack your knuckles? | A gas bubble forming in the joint fluid | The bones breaking a tiny bit and mending again | Two bones grinding together | A muscle snapping back
exercise: Which animal's wing has the same arm bones as your arm? | A bat | A butterfly | A honeybee | A dragonfly
test: Count them: one hand has 27 bones. How many bones are there in both hands? | 54 | 27 | 44 | 108
test: Count them: a hand has 5 bones in the palm and 14 in the fingers. How many bones does the hand have without the wrist? | 19 | 27 | 14 | 9
test: Mickey Mouse is drawn with only four fingers on each hand. How many fingers do real people have on both hands, thumbs included? | 10 | 8 | 12 | 16
test: In a Tom and Jerry cartoon Tom's arm is tied in a knot and then he shakes it straight. What is true about a real arm? | It has stiff bones joined at the shoulder, elbow and wrist, so it cannot tie in a knot | It is boneless and bends anywhere | It has one long bone from shoulder to fingertips | Its bones bend like rubber in children
test: «banged me elbow, me pinky's tingling, did I break a bone» What is most likely? | You hit the nerve called the funny bone; the tingling soon stops | Yes, you snapped the funny bone clean in half and it needs a plaster cast | The little finger is broken | You hit a vein in the finger
test: «fell on my hand off the bike, now my shoulder hurts, that's weird innit» What is the best answer? | A fall on an outstretched arm sends the force up and can break the collarbone, so see a doctor | Shoulders cannot be hurt by falling on a hand | Only the hand can be hurt in such a fall | It is just tiredness from cycling
test: «cracking knuckles gives you arthritis, my nan says» What is fairest to say? | Studies have not shown that it causes arthritis | Doctors have proven again and again that it causes arthritis | It breaks the knuckle bones | It makes your fingers grow longer
test: Why does it take a while before you can crack the same knuckle again? | The gas bubble needs time to dissolve back into the fluid | The tiny crack in the bone needs time to heal first | The muscle is too tired | The joint locks for a day
test: A doctor sees only a few wrist bones on a child's X-ray, though the child is ten. What might that show? | The child's bones are growing slower than usual | The child has lost some wrist bones in an accident long ago | The X-ray machine is broken | The child is older than ten
test: What would be hard to do if your thumb could not touch your fingertips? | Pick up a small coin or hold a pencil well | Swing your whole arm round in a big circle | Bend your elbow | Stand on one leg
test: Which arm joint can swing in a full circle? | The shoulder | The elbow | The knuckle | The wrist
exam: Identify the bone of the forearm situated upon the side of the thumb. | The radius | The ulna | The humerus | The scapula
exam: State the number of bones of which a single human hand is composed, the wrist included. | Twenty-seven | Fourteen | Five | Fifty-four
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the so-called funny bone? | It is a nerve passing the elbow, and not a bone | It is the distal extremity of the humerus | It is a minute bone within the elbow | It is a muscle of the forearm
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the shoulder joint. | It is a ball-and-socket joint, the most mobile of the body, with a shallow socket | It is a hinge joint permitting motion in one plane alone | It is a fused joint incapable of motion | It is the most stable joint of the body
